Q('c1','Benzoic acid dimerises','A solute dimerises by 88%. Its van ’t Hoff factor is:','0.56',['0.88','1.88','0.12'],'Dimerisation changes particle count by i = 1 − α/2 = 1 − 0.44.')
Q('c1','Use boiling elevation to find freezing depression','An aqueous glucose solution has ΔT_b = 0.20 K. With K_b = 0.512 and K_f = 1.86, its freezing point is approximately:','−0.727°C',['+0.727°C','−0.055°C','−1.86°C'],'For the same solution, ΔT_f/ΔT_b = K_f/K_b. ΔT_f = 0.7266 K.')
Q('c1','Phenol mixed with aniline','Strong hydrogen bonding between phenol and aniline favours:','Negative deviation from Raoult’s law',['Positive deviation from Raoult’s law','Ideal behaviour regardless of composition','An increase in vapour pressure above the ideal value'],'Stronger unlike attractions reduce escaping tendency and vapour pressure.')
Q('c1','Compare gas solubility','With Henry’s law written p = K_Hx, greater gas solubility at fixed pressure corresponds to:','A smaller K_H',['A larger K_H','K_H always equal to zero','K_H independent of gas identity'],'x = p/K_H, so dissolved mole fraction increases as K_H decreases.')
Q('c2','Combine two standard couples','E°(Sn⁴⁺/Sn²⁺) = 0.15 V and E°(Cr³⁺/Cr) = −0.74 V. The spontaneous cell EMF is:','0.89 V',['−0.89 V','0.59 V','1.78 V'],'Subtract the anode reduction potential from the cathode reduction potential: 0.15 − (−0.74).')
Q('c2','Concentration changes in a Daniell cell','For Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu, increasing [Cu²⁺] alone makes cell EMF:','Increase',['Decrease','Become exactly zero','Stay unchanged at every concentration'],'The reaction quotient [Zn²⁺]/[Cu²⁺] falls, so E = E° − (0.0591/2)logQ rises.')
Q('c2','Calibrate a conductivity cell','A conductivity cell has κ = 0.0135 S cm⁻¹ and R = 100 Ω. Its cell constant is:','1.35 cm⁻¹',['0.000135 cm⁻¹','135 cm⁻¹','1.35 cm'],'Cell constant = κR = 0.0135 × 100 = 1.35 cm⁻¹.')
Q('c2','Limiting molar conductivity','Given λ°(Mg²⁺) = 106 and λ°(Cl⁻) = 76.3 S cm² mol⁻¹, Λ°(MgCl₂) is:','258.6 S cm² mol⁻¹',['182.3 S cm² mol⁻¹','288.3 S cm² mol⁻¹','152.6 S cm² mol⁻¹'],'Kohlrausch’s law gives 106 + 2 × 76.3; count both chloride ions.')
Q('c3','From half-life to 90% completion','A first-order reaction has half-life 40 min. Its time for 90% completion is approximately:','133 min',['80 min','72 min','360 min'],'t = t_½ log10/log2 = 40/0.3010 ≈ 132.9 min.')
Q('c3','25% versus 75% completion','A first-order reaction reaches 25% completion in 20 min. Total time for 75% completion is about:','96.4 min',['60 min','40 min','76.4 min'],'The remaining fractions are 0.75 and 0.25; t₂/t₁ = ln4/ln(4/3).')
Q('c3','Activation energy from two half-lives','A first-order reaction’s half-life changes from 20 min at 300 K to 5 min at 350 K. E_a is approximately:','24.2 kJ mol⁻¹',['2.42 kJ mol⁻¹','242 kJ mol⁻¹','−24.2 kJ mol⁻¹'],'k₂/k₁ = 4. Use E_a = R ln4/(1/300 − 1/350).')
Q('c3','What does a catalyst change?','A catalyst changes which quantity for a reaction?','Activation energy',['Standard Gibbs energy change','Equilibrium constant at fixed temperature','Energy difference between reactants and products'],'It supplies a lower-barrier pathway without changing reaction thermodynamics.')
Q('c4','Spin-only moment of Cr³⁺','The spin-only moment of Cr³⁺ is approximately:','3.87 BM',['1.73 BM','2.83 BM','5.92 BM'],'Cr³⁺ is d³ with three unpaired electrons: μ = √[3(3+2)] BM.')
Q('c4','Why Zn²⁺ salts are colourless','Zn²⁺ lacks ordinary d–d colour mainly because it has:','A completely filled 3d subshell',['An empty 3d subshell','Five unpaired d electrons','A partially occupied 4s subshell'],'Zn²⁺ is [Ar]3d¹⁰. This statement concerns d–d transitions, not every possible source of colour.')
Q('c4','Why separating Zr and Hf is difficult','The close similarity of Zr and Hf radii is largely due to:','Lanthanoid contraction',['Excellent shielding by 4f electrons','Identical atomic numbers','Absence of any 4f electrons in the intervening series'],'Poor 4f shielding causes contraction before Hf, bringing its radius close to Zr’s.')
Q('c4','Why transition metals catalyse reactions','Which property helps many transition metals act as catalysts?','Variable oxidation states and intermediate formation',['An inability to bind reactants','An invariant oxidation state for every metal','Completely filled d orbitals in every ion'],'Accessible oxidation states and complex formation can open lower-energy reaction pathways.')
Q('c5','Two Ni²⁺ complexes behave differently','Which description of [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ is correct?','Square planar and diamagnetic',['Tetrahedral and paramagnetic','Octahedral and diamagnetic','Square planar with two unpaired electrons'],'Strong-field CN⁻ pairs d⁸ electrons; the standard VBT description is dsp², square planar.')
Q('c5','Why low-spin tetrahedral complexes are rare','Tetrahedral complexes are usually high spin because:','Δ_t is generally smaller than pairing energy',['Δ_t is always larger than pairing energy','Tetrahedral orbitals never split','Every tetrahedral metal ion is d⁰'],'Electrons preferentially occupy higher orbitals singly rather than pair.')
Q('c5','Use the AgNO₃ test to find the sphere','CoCl₃·6NH₃ yields three moles of AgCl per mole in the usual test. Its coordination formula is:','[Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃',['[Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl₂·NH₃','[Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]Cl·2NH₃','[Co(NH₃)₃Cl₃]·3NH₃'],'Three immediately precipitable chlorides lie outside the coordination sphere.')
Q('c5','Distinguish ionisation isomers','Which complex has sulfate as a free counterion?','[Co(NH₃)₅Cl]SO₄',['[Co(NH₃)₅SO₄]Cl','[Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃','[Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]Cl'],'Species outside square brackets are counterions; free sulfate gives BaSO₄ with a suitable barium salt.')
Q('c6','Most SN1-reactive C₄H₉Br isomer','Which C₄H₉Br isomer most readily forms a carbocation for SN1?','2-Bromo-2-methylpropane',['1-Bromobutane','1-Bromo-2-methylpropane','2-Bromobutane'],'The tertiary carbocation is more stabilised than the primary and secondary alternatives.')
Q('c6','Configuration in an SN2 reaction','An SN2 reaction at a stereogenic carbon causes:','Inversion of configuration',['Complete retention','Carbocation rearrangement as an obligatory step','Racemisation through a planar intermediate'],'The nucleophile attacks from the back as the leaving group departs.')
Q('c6','Why haloarenes resist nucleophilic substitution','A major reason chlorobenzene resists ordinary nucleophilic substitution is:','Partial double-bond character of its C–Cl bond',['A particularly stable free phenyl carbocation','A weaker C–Cl bond than every alkyl chloride','An sp³ carbon attached to chlorine'],'Resonance strengthens the aryl C–Cl bond; the bonded carbon is sp².')
Q('c6','Alcoholic KOH with 2-bromopentane','The major alkene from heating 2-bromopentane with alcoholic KOH is:','Pent-2-ene',['Pent-1-ene','Pentane','Pentan-2-ol'],'β-Elimination favours the more substituted alkene under these conditions.')
Q('c7','Why phenol is more acidic than alcohol','Phenol is more acidic than ethanol chiefly because:','Phenoxide is resonance-stabilised',['Ethoxide is more resonance-stabilised','Phenol cannot hydrogen bond','Phenol has no O–H bond'],'The conjugate base phenoxide disperses charge; ethoxide lacks comparable resonance stabilisation.')
Q('c7','Nitration of phenol under mild conditions','Dilute HNO₃ at low temperature converts phenol mainly into:','A mixture of ortho- and para-nitrophenol',['Only meta-nitrophenol','Only 2,4,6-trinitrophenol','Nitrobenzene'],'The –OH group activates the ring and directs nitration to ortho and para positions.')
Q('c7','Reimer–Tiemann reaction','Phenol with CHCl₃/aqueous NaOH, followed by acidification, gives mainly:','2-Hydroxybenzaldehyde',['Benzoic acid','Anisole','Acetophenone'],'Reimer–Tiemann introduces –CHO predominantly ortho to –OH.')
Q('c7','Phenol to anisole','Which pair is suitable for preparing anisole by Williamson synthesis?','Sodium phenoxide and methyl iodide',['Chlorobenzene and sodium methoxide','Phenol and aqueous HCl','Sodium benzoate and methane'],'Methyl iodide is an unhindered SN2 substrate for phenoxide.')
Q('c8','Identify the C₅H₁₀O compound','A C₅H₁₀O compound gives DNP and iodoform tests, not Tollens’, and oxidises to ethanoic and propanoic acids. It is:','Pentan-2-one',['Pentan-3-one','Pentanal','Cyclopentanol'],'The tests indicate a methyl ketone; the oxidation fragments identify pentan-2-one.')
Q('c8','Distinguish butanal and butan-2-one','With Tollens’ reagent, which observation distinguishes butanal from butan-2-one?','Butanal gives a silver mirror; butan-2-one does not',['Both give silver mirrors','Only butan-2-one gives a silver mirror','Neither reacts under the usual test conditions'],'Butanal is an aldehyde and is oxidised by Tollens’ reagent; the simple ketone is not.')
Q('c8','Benzaldehyde with concentrated alkali','Benzaldehyde with concentrated NaOH produces:','Benzyl alcohol and sodium benzoate',['Phenol and sodium ethanoate','Only an aldol addition product','Benzene and methanol'],'Without an α-hydrogen, benzaldehyde undergoes Cannizzaro disproportionation.')
Q('c8','Ethanal to but-2-enal','Ethanal forms but-2-enal through:','Aldol addition followed by dehydration',['Cannizzaro reaction only','Reduction followed by esterification','Nitration followed by elimination'],'Dilute base gives 3-hydroxybutanal; heating removes water to give but-2-enal.')
Q('c9','Methylamine versus aniline','Aniline is less basic than methylamine mainly because its nitrogen lone pair is:','Delocalised into the aromatic ring',['Absent from the molecule','Permanently removed by methyl groups','Localised more strongly than in methylamine'],'Resonance makes the lone pair less available for protonation.')
Q('c9','Why aniline fails Friedel–Crafts conditions','Aniline does not undergo ordinary Friedel–Crafts reaction with AlCl₃ because:','Its nitrogen coordinates to AlCl₃ and deactivates the ring',['It contains no aromatic ring','AlCl₃ converts it directly into benzene','The amino group has no lone pair'],'Complex formation with the Lewis acid suppresses the expected ring substitution.')
Q('c9','Propanamide to propanamine','Which reagent converts propanamide to propanamine while retaining all carbon atoms?','LiAlH₄ followed by work-up',['Br₂/NaOH','NaNO₂/HCl','Tollens’ reagent'],'Amide reduction retains the carbonyl carbon; Hofmann degradation loses it.')
Q('c9','Carbylamine test','The carbylamine test is given by:','Primary aliphatic and aromatic amines',['Only secondary amines','Only tertiary amines','All amines regardless of substitution'],'Primary amines react with CHCl₃ and alcoholic KOH to form isocyanides.')
Q('c10','Lactose and maltose hydrolysis','Hydrolysis of lactose yields:','Glucose and galactose',['Glucose and fructose','Two fructose molecules','Two galactose molecules'],'Lactose contains glucose and galactose; sucrose instead yields glucose and fructose.')
Q('c10','Glucose with two oxidising reagents','Bromine water oxidises glucose to:','Gluconic acid',['Saccharic acid','Glucitol','Fructose'],'This mild oxidant converts the aldehyde group to –COOH; concentrated HNO₃ oxidises both ends.')
Q('c10','Native versus denatured protein','Ordinary protein denaturation generally preserves:','Primary amino-acid sequence',['All tertiary folding','Full biological activity','Every hydrogen bond in the native structure'],'Denaturation disrupts higher-order structure without generally hydrolysing peptide bonds.')
Q('c10','Peptide and glycosidic linkages','The linkage joining amino-acid residues in a protein is:','–CO–NH–',['–O–O–','–CH=CH–','–N=N–'],'A peptide bond is an amide bond between amino and carboxyl groups.')
